What Actually Needs Attention at 180,000 kmIf this car breaks down in the next 5 years, it will almost certainly not be a
dead engine block. It will likely be inexpensive wear-and-tear parts. To keep it healthy, watch out for these common 2010
Mazda 3 maintenance items:The Passenger Motor Mount: This is a notorious fluid-filled rubber mount that rips over time.
If your car vibrates heavily at idle, the engine is fine—you just need a new $60 rubber mount.Suspension Bushings: At 
this mileage, the rubber parts in the rear suspension and front control arms dry out and crack, causing squeaks or loose
handling.Rust Prevention: Depending on where you live, rust on the rear wheel arches and undercarriage will kill a 2010 
Mazda 3 long before the engine gives up.Thermostat & Cooling: The factory thermostat can stick open around this mileage,
causing the car to take a long time to warm up. It is a cheap, easy fix.

